---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ToolHero from '../components/ToolHero.astro';
import { tool } from '../data/tools';

const vivary = tool('vivary');
---

<Base
  title="Vivary"
  description="A desktop workspace for working with AI agents on your own projects. Agent chat, project files, tools, and memory in one app. Windows first, self-hostable, and everything stays on your machine."
>
  <ToolHero tool={vivary} />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap two-col">
      <div>
        <h2 class="heading">What it is</h2>
        <p>
          One app where you open a project, talk to an agent about it, and watch what
          the agent does to your files. The agents, your credentials, your files, and
          the conversation history stay on the machine that runs Vivary.
        </p>
      </div>
      <dl class="lanes">
        <div class="lane">
          <dt>Work on a project</dt>
          <dd>
            Open a project and work with an agent that can read files, edit them, and run
            tools. Every change lands in your own folder.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="lane">
          <dt>Come back to it</dt>
          <dd>
            Return to a project's conversations, search past sessions, and keep the
            memory worth keeping in files you can open.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="lane">
          <dt>Use the harnesses you have</dt>
          <dd>
            Supported coding harnesses you already installed, such as Claude Code, run
            inside the workspace with their own tools. Guidance for them lives in files
            in the project.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="lane">
          <dt>See what you're building</dt>
          <dd>
            Preview the site or dashboard the agent is working on, and let the agent
            inspect and debug it.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="lane">
          <dt>Reach it from your phone</dt>
          <dd>
            Run the instance on your computer or a server of your own, and connect a
            phone browser to it when you choose to turn on remote access.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap two-col">
      <div>
        <h2 class="heading">What it asks of you</h2>
      </div>
      <dl class="lanes">
        <div class="lane">
          <dt>No Vivary account</dt>
          <dd>Local desktop use needs none. Your provider accounts are yours and separate.</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="lane">
          <dt>Remote access is opt-in</dt>
          <dd>Off until you set it up, and authenticated when it's on.</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="lane">
          <dt>Windows first</dt>
          <dd>The desktop app targets Windows. A Mac build is possible later work, not a promise.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="heading">Status</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        In development. A Windows candidate has been tested privately and is not a public
        release. The <a href={vivary.primary.href}>release queue</a> lists what is verified
        and what is still required, in the open. MIT.
      </p>
      <p class="original">
        The original Vivary command-line tools, which scaffold typed memory for agent
        workspaces, are preserved as <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@vivary/create">@vivary/create</a> on npm and <a href="https://pypi.org/project/create-vivary/">create-vivary</a> on PyPI. They are not the desktop app.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .original {
    margin-top: 28px;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
</style>
